("3. If no Suspense boundary is found: in a concurrent root, a suspended render **without** a boundary around it. A root-level suspension during an initial sync update is an error in older versions (\"A component suspended while responding to synchronous input\") unless in a transition. In React 19, the guidance is to wrap in a boundary or use a transition (the sync/transition distinction still determines whether the *whole root* can wait). ⚠ This area changed; verify for your version.",
 "3. If no Suspense boundary is found: in a concurrent root (the only kind in React 19) the render simply **suspends without committing**, waiting indefinitely for the thenable; the previous UI stays and the update is retried when the thenable settles. The old error \"A component suspended while responding to synchronous input\" is now thrown only for *legacy* roots, which React 19 removed (`ReactFiberThrow.js` on `main`: \"In a concurrent root, suspending without a Suspense boundary is allowed. It will suspend indefinitely without committing.\"). Executed on react 19.3.1: `root.render(<Suspender/>)` with no boundary rendered nothing until the promise resolved, and a `flushSync(() => setState(...))` update that suspended with no boundary neither threw nor committed until resolution. Practical advice is unchanged (add a boundary or use a transition so the user sees something sensible), but do not expect that error message in React 19."),
("- Component **state in the hidden primary tree is preserved** (the components are not unmounted). ⚠ Based on the design of Offscreen-style hidden trees; verify behavior for your version with an experiment (§5.5).\n- **Layout effects are cleaned up** on hide and re-run on reveal (documented). Passive-effect behavior while hidden is implementation-specific; verify.",
 "- Component **state in the hidden primary tree is preserved** (the components are not unmounted). Executed on react 19.3.1: state set to `7` before an urgent re-suspend was still `7` after reveal, and the `useState` initializer ran once; the hidden DOM carried `style=\"display: none !important\"` (also see §5.5).\n- **Layout effects are cleaned up** on hide and re-run on reveal (documented). Executed on 19.3.1 with `[]`-dependency effects: **passive effects were *not* cleaned up** when Suspense hid the content (they stay mounted), unlike `<Activity mode=\"hidden\">`, which cleans up both. That passive-effect behavior is an implementation detail, not documented."),
("### 3.6 Sibling behavior: React 18 vs 19 ⚠","### 3.6 Sibling behavior: React 18 vs 19"),
("Retries are scheduled in their own low-priority lanes ([14](./14-lanes.md)) so they don't interrupt typing. Documented: reveals are throttled to once per 300 ms so the UI doesn't flash multiple tiny reveals; boundaries ready within the window appear together. ⚠ Exact values and heuristics are implementation tuning; the documented sentence is the most you should rely on.",
 "Retries are scheduled in their own low-priority lanes ([14](./14-lanes.md)) so they don't interrupt typing. Documented: reveals are throttled to once per 300 ms so the UI doesn't flash multiple tiny reveals; boundaries ready within the window appear together. This is observable: in the sandbox a promise that resolved at 100 ms was not revealed until about 310 ms after mount (see §4.1). The constant is `FALLBACK_THROTTLE_MS = 300` in `ReactFiberWorkLoop.js`; the documented sentence is the most you should rely on."),
